{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788354838,"nonce":"NXILlpL5fOYeP//aqLVqBvbbiaM=","message":"**Implemented, worker, repair round 2026-09-02.** Branch\n`fix/a1644d6-enclosure`, commit `30b7608`. Source only — no assembly committed;\nthe orchestrator builds on `main`. Not run against a bench by me (workers never\nlaunch anything).\n\n\n`LayoutEnclosure` in `Source/AutoRimmer/PlaceVerbs.cs`, called from three\nplaces, plus `LayoutEnclosureWatch` for the age.\n\n**Both flags, always, per acceptance item 1.** `enclosed` is\n`Verse/Room.ProperRoom`; `uses_outdoor_temp` is `UsesOutdoorTemperature`. They\nare aggregated over every room the layout's declared interior actually resolves\nto and republished per room (`enclosure.rooms[]` carries `proper`,\n`uses_outdoor_temp`, `open_roof_cells`, `touches_map_edge`, `temp_c`).\n`PsychologicallyOutdoors` is untouched and still rides on `indoors`.\n\n**The gap is named, per item 2 — and the per-cell test is not the obvious one.**\n`enclosure.gaps[]` carries `at`, `def`, `is_door`, `placement_id`, `standing`\n(`blueprint` | `frame` | `missing` | `other-building`) and `region_type`.\n`enclosure.unroofed[]` names the roofless declared-interior cells. Both are\ncapped at 12 with `gaps_more` / `unroofed_more` complete.\n\n**The per-cell test is `Verse/RegionTypeUtility.GetExpectedRegionType`, NOT\n`GetEdifice`, and this is the find worth having from the implementation.**\n`RimWorld/ThingDefGenerator_Buildings.NewFrameDef_Thing` does\n`thingDef.building.isEdifice = def.building.isEdifice`, so **a wall frame is an\nedifice and registers in `Verse/EdificeGrid`** — `c.GetEdifice(map)` is non-null\nat a half-built wall. The same generator clamps `passability` from `Impassable`\nto `PassThroughOnly` and sets `fillPercent = 0.2f`, so the cell is walkable and\nthe room leaks straight through it. Keying the gap check on \"an edifice stands\nhere\" would have reported a half-built wall as SEALED, which is the original\ndefect with extra steps. Calling the region builder's own predicate instead\nmakes the comment's nuance fall out rather than be special-cased: a built door\nis `Portal` and closes the room, an unbuilt one is `Normal` and does not, a\nframe is `Normal`, and a sandbag in a wall slot is `ImpassableFreeAirExchange`\nand reads open, correctly.\n\n**Reachable from a turn-level read, per item 3, on two surfaces.** `rooms`\ngains `layouts_unenclosed` (plus `layouts_total` / `layouts_checked` /\n`layouts_failing` / `layouts_cap`) — an EMPTY list is published, because an\nabsent key and \"nothing wrong\" must not look alike. `digest.construction` gains\nthe same rows under presence-is-the-signal. The digest is deliberate and is\nthe bar this issue set (\"the agent is told **without asking cell-by-cell**\"): a\nkey only `construction {layout_id}` carried would still need the agent to\nsuspect the layout first, which is exactly what the run never did. Note that\n`rooms` could never have listed the freezer itself — it enumerates rooms that\nEXIST and skips `PsychologicallyOutdoors` ones, so an unclosed freezer's cells\nare in the outdoor blob it filters out.\n\n**Item 4, the day boundary.** `f9dadc7` is not shipped, so \"read consistently\"\nwas met by adopting its settled field idiom verbatim:\n`enclosure.unenclosed_for` = `{since_tick, ticks, day_boundaries,\ntracked_since, stale}` plus a `floor_note` when the layout was already open at\nthe first read this process made. In memory, cleared in\n`Runtime.ResetForGameBoundary` beside `Placements`/`Layouts` — no scribed state\n(`d16a463`). Sampled at read time, not ticked, and the envelope says the age is\na floor. When a layout stops failing the block disappears rather than zeroing.\n\n**Item 5.** `checklists/turn.md` gains `room-that-is-not-a-room`;\n`templates/freezer-kitchen.md` gains a step 0 that reads both flags before\nanything is wired, on the template that shipped this failure; the playbook\nlesson (`playbook/a-room-is-not-a-room-until-the-game-says-so.md`) is cited by\nboth and indexed.\n\n**Item 6.** `accept/a1644d6-enclosure.py`, 94 checks across 7 bench phases.\n\n\n**1. \"Intended roofed\" is the DECLARED INTERIOR, not the IR's roof mask —\nbecause `place-layout` never receives the mask.** The comment above says the\nintended roof is \"the IR's own `roof` mask, which every shipped template already\ncarries\", and the templates do carry one — but `LayoutVerbs`' header records the\nroof as a deliberate non-consumption (\"a roof is a DESIGNATION, not a\nplacement\"), and `place-layout` takes a resolved element list. Adding a `roof`\nargument would change that verb's contract to serve a report, so I did not. The\ncells the declared shell encloses are used instead: for a room layout it is the\nsame set, it needs no new IR field, and it is the set `UsesOutdoorTemperature`\ncounts against. Where the built room is bigger than the declared interior (two\nlayouts sharing a wall), the room's own `OpenRoofCount` is published beside the\nnamed holes and `unroofed_note` says which is the floor.\n\n**2. \"Intends a room\" is decided from the DECLARATION alone, and that is the\ncry-wolf guard.** The roll-ups need \"which placed layouts should be rooms and\nare not\", and the tempting test — has walls, interior is not proper — reports a\ndefensive wall or a conduit spine as a failed room forever. So: a 4-connected\nflood over the layout's own rect blocked by the declared `Wall`/`Door` cells; a\ncomponent that never reaches the rect perimeter is a declared interior.\n**No game state in it** — same answer on day 1 and day 40 — bounded by the rect,\nand it is the file's only search; enclosure itself is still `ProperRoom`. A\nlayout that declares no room gets `enclosed: null`, never `false`, and never\nappears in a roll-up.\n\nVerified offline over every shipped template (`--selftest`, 16 checks):\n`freezer-kitchen` declares 2 rooms / 32 interior cells behind 34 shell cells,\n`bedroom` 1 / 15 / 20, `power-room` 1 / 25 / 24, and a 5-cell wall run declares\n0. **The freezer template is only checkable because `Cooler` is `fillPercent\n1.0` / `Impassable` in Core's `Buildings_Temperature.xml` and seals a wall slot\nexactly as `Wall` does** — had it not been, the flood would escape through the\nnorth wall and the one template that shipped this failure would have been\nsilently exempt from the whole check. `Heater` is 0.4 / `PassThroughOnly` and is\ncorrectly not shell.\n\n\n`accept/a1644d6-enclosure.py` — phases 0-6 on a bench, 9 offline.\n\n- **Phase 1 stages mechanism 2 ALONE.** An instant-mode room is sealed the\n  moment it is placed and completely unroofed (the roof is colonist work), so\n  `enclosed:true` and `uses_outdoor_temp:true` arrive in one envelope with no\n  wall gap anywhere.\n- **Phase 2 stages mechanism 1 alone** by `dev:destroy` on one declared wall\n  cell: `enclosed:false`, `open_shell_cells:1`, `gaps[0].at` the exact cell,\n  `standing:\"missing\"`, `region_type:\"Normal\"`, and the interior now in a room\n  ten times the layout's size that `touches_map_edge`. It then asserts the\n  layout appears in `rooms.layouts_unenclosed` with `first_gap.at` and in\n  `digest.construction.layouts_unenclosed`.\n- **Phase 3 restores the wall and asserts `enclosed` flips true while\n  `uses_outdoor_temp` stays true** — the second mechanism observable with the\n  first satisfied, which is the acceptance bullet, on one room, one call apart.\n- **Phase 4 lets the colony roof it** and reads `room {id}` on the result:\n  proper, indoors, `uses_outdoor_temp:false`, `open_roof_cells:0`, a real\n  `temp_c`. That is the sealed roofed room `261f2e9` phase 6 has never had, and\n  the script prints the room id to hand over.\n- **Phase 5** is a blueprint-mode layout: `intends_room:true` with nothing\n  built, all 20 shell cells open, `standing:\"blueprint\"`, and the unbuilt DOOR\n  cell present as a gap flagged `is_door`.\n- **Phase 6 is the cry-wolf guard**: a straight 5-cell wall must report\n  `intends_room:false`, `enclosed:null`, and must never appear in the roll-up.\n- **Phase 9 (`--selftest`, offline, 16 checks, PASSES today)** runs the\n  declared-interior rule over the shipped templates and closes the\n  absent-vs-null trap against a reconstructed pre-fix envelope carrying\n  `done:true` and no enclosure block.\n\nI did NOT demonstrate any of phases 0-6 — no bench was launched.\n\n\nThe gap check keys on what stands at the cell, so \"destroyed by `dev:destroy`\",\n\"cancelled\" and \"never built\" are the same reading — which is the design, and\nis why phase 2 destroys a wall rather than omitting an element. Omitting one\nwould leave the cell UNDECLARED, and an undeclared cell is not a gap; it is a\nlayout that never asked for a wall there.","files":null}]}